Subject: [PATCH 02/11] PS1-RAM step 1: island and free-play callbacks leave
 resident RAM (+21,808 B)

ovl_plan.py kept a function resident as soon as its address was taken. 22 KB
of code were resident for addresses that never leave their own group:

- fp_island_gfx.c: the six draw_*_body functions go to its own lr_run(),
  which runs them at once; lr_tap sits in ui_raster's ur_tap only around one
  ui_fill_circle / ui_fill_ellipse call (LOCAL_CALLBACKS). 10.6 KB of the
  island's drawing move into the island group (58 -> 69 KB of the 131 KB
  slot, plus its 20 KB of scene scratch).
- fp_title.c: pc_pill / pc_in / pc_vile go to its own logo_snap().
- modal callbacks that were not listed yet (MODAL_CALLBACKS): the citizen
  page's cb_manage (with do_action and everything it opens, 6 KB), the
  memorial's mem_flowers, the title's finished_pick / fresh_world_go, the
  profile row's pf_leave / pf_renamed / pf_join.
- new PICK_CALLBACKS: a callback a free-play screen gives to the citizen
  picker is called by the picker's own update, with the free-play group in
  the slot (wz_neighbour, cb_introduce). The planner checks that the
  function's group is the picker's; the island's picker callbacks are not
  listed and stay resident.

The free-play group grows from 106 to 117 KB; the slot (131,072 B, set by
the house and the story) is unchanged. No source file changes: the same
code runs from the slot instead of from resident RAM.

Game disc with the boot FMV: 65,936 -> 87,744 B below the stack top.

test_tools.py: the planner on a made-up game of four scenes (which callbacks
may leave, which must stay).

